+    // [NeoN faithful-port patch -- NOT upstream] OpenFOAM's GAMGSolver::scale (GAMGSolverScale.C)
+    // finishes its Rayleigh correction with ONE INLINE DIAGONAL DIVISION:
+    //     field[i] = sf*field[i] + (source[i] - sf*Acf[i]) / D[i];
+    // i.e. the inner operator is exactly D^-1 -- one divide per cell, no operator application.
+    // Ginkgo's port instead calls `pre_smoother->apply()`, the full configured smoother. For the
+    // standard Ir(1){Schwarz{Jacobi(1)}} smoother that pays an EXTRA residual SpMV -- a distributed
+    // HALO EXCHANGE per correction point, per level, per V-cycle -- that OpenFOAM never spends. That
+    // single infidelity roughly DOUBLES scale correction's SpMV cost, and SpMV halos were measured as
+    // 77% of its total communication.
+    //
+    // Ir's INNER solver *is* that D^-1 (the Schwarz{Jacobi} it wraps), so applying it directly is both
+    // faithful to OpenFOAM and SpMV-free. It also drops Ir's relaxation_factor, which is correct:
+    // OpenFOAM's scale() applies no relaxation. Non-Ir smoothers fall back to the original path.
+    // RELAXATION (2026-07-15 fix -- do NOT remove): Ir applies `x += relaxation_factor * error`,
+    // so its inner solver alone is the UNDAMPED D^-1. Dropping the damping was justified as "OpenFOAM's
+    // scale() has no relaxation" -- true of OpenFOAM, but OpenFOAM's smoothers are its own (GaussSeidel
+    // by default), whereas Ginkgo's Ir(0.9){Jacobi} DEPENDS on that damping for stability. Undamped it
+    // overshoots: at max_levels=2 (coarse grid still ~12M rows, so the correction is large) convergence
+    // collapsed 18.3 -> 122.4 iterations, while L4 was unaffected -- the classic signature of an
